"""Preparation-only reference solution for ``flag_gems.linalg_vander``.

This file is injected unchanged by the native FlagGems pytest option

    --override linalg_vander:<this file>:run

so the very same file is the "candidate" the review probe observes and the
reference semantics behind both suites. It is a preparation oracle, not an
optimised kernel, and it does not import ``flag_gems`` (the injected export is
never used as its own oracle).

Entry ABI follows the frozen definition (``definitions/linalg_vander.json``):

    run(x, N=None) -> out

* positional/keyword ``x`` (required), optional keyword ``N`` defaulting to
  ``None`` -> ``x.shape[-1]``, exactly like ``torch.linalg.vander``;
* ``out.shape == x.shape[:-1] + (x.shape[-1], N)``;
* pure function: no mutation of ``x``, no alias/shared storage with ``x``
  (matches ``effects: {"mutates": [], "returns_alias_of": {}}``);
* output dtype equals the input dtype, output device equals ``x.device``.

Semantics:

* ``torch.linalg.vander`` is the authoritative native oracle and natively
  supports float32/float64/complex inputs, so those dtypes are delegated
  directly (the accuracy test computes its expectation with the same call).
* The native op rejects float16/bfloat16 (the accuracy test itself upcasts the
  reference input for those dtypes). They are therefore evaluated in float32 --
  the same compute dtype the frozen FlagGems kernel uses for every non-float64
  output, "libdevice pow is not accurate at low precision" -- and cast back to
  the incoming dtype so the returned dtype still equals the input dtype.
"""

import torch


def run(x, N=None):
    dtype = x.dtype
    if dtype in (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16):
        wide = torch.linalg.vander(x.to(torch.float32), N=N)
        return wide.to(dtype)
    return torch.linalg.vander(x, N=N)
